This confirms the existing scaffold's `FixedResourceProvider` workaround for classpath resource enumeration under Native Image — exactly the concern the prior automation attempt flagged in its guidance. I have enough evidence now to finalize the decision.

## Findings

The `tests/src/org.flywaydb/flyway-sqlserver/11.8.1` scaffold already contains an mssql-jdbc test dependency, a pinned `mssql/server` Docker image, and a `FixedResourceProvider` workaround for classpath resource enumeration. A prior automation run on this exact issue (preserved on branch `ai/kimeta/human-intervention/issue-9843-...`, visible via `git log`) reached the identical preflight conclusion — same dependency, same Docker image/slug — and that setup applied and built successfully (`setupDone: true`); the run later stalled for unrelated reasons (a "reporter-requested metadata" finalization step), not because of setup or a Native Image incompatibility. Separately, `org.flywaydb:flyway-core:13.5.0` (the base library) was already merged as supported under Native Image (`79917b7ed30`), so the SQL Server extension isn't inherently unsupportable. `flyway-sqlserver:13.5.0`'s POM declares no JDBC driver dependency (same as 11.8.1 — it's optional/BYO), and the mssql-jdbc/jackson-databind versions already pinned in the scaffold remain compatible (Flyway's own dependencyManagement pins mssql-jdbc 12.10.2.jre11 and legacy jackson-databind 2.22.2, but those aren't forced onto consumers). No mechanism (runtime codegen, agent self-attach, custom classloading, etc.) is present in the failure evidence — the issue log is truncated before any actual runtime error, so the unsupportable checklist fails at step 1.

